## `_resolveProviderConfig` (session-manager.ts:1802–1832)

```ts
private _resolveProviderConfig(model?: string): Record<string, any> {
    // 1. Try the multi-provider registry
    const registry = this.workerDefaults.modelProviders;
    if (registry) {
        const resolved = registry.resolve(model);
        if (resolved) {
            if (resolved.type === "github") {
                return {};
            }
            if (resolved.sdkProvider) {
                return { provider: resolved.sdkProvider };
            }
        }
    }

    // 2. Fall back to legacy single provider
    const p = this.workerDefaults.provider;
    if (!p) return {};

    // For Azure, dynamically construct deployment URL
    if (p.type === "azure" && model && !p.baseUrl.includes("/deployments/")) {
        return {
            provider: {
                ...p,
                baseUrl: `${p.baseUrl.replace(/\/+$/, "")}/deployments/${model}`,
            },
        };
    }
    return { provider: p };
}
```

The function has exactly the two paths you described, and both special-case Azure but in slightly different ways.

### 1. Registry path (lines 1803–1816)

- Detection/matching: it delegates entirely to `this.workerDefaults.modelProviders.resolve(model)` (`ModelProviderRegistry.resolve`, model-providers.ts:258–295). That method normalizes `model` (`provider:model` or bare name) to a qualified name, looks up the matching `ModelProviderConfig`, and returns a `ResolvedProvider` whose `type` field is one of `"github" | "azure" | "openai" | "anthropic"`. So azure-detection happens *inside* the registry, keyed on the `type: "azure"` field of the matched provider config entry, not in `_resolveProviderConfig` itself.
